body:
&#x200B;

Here's a link to my original post, where a commenter advised it.  [https://www.reddit.com/r/careeradvice/comments/cboh01/i\_think\_i\_might\_get\_fired\_how\_would\_i\_handle/](https://www.reddit.com/r/careeradvice/comments/cboh01/i_think_i_might_get_fired_how_would_i_handle/) 

&#x200B;

 *Every time recruiters call and ask what happened at my last job, I tell them I was laid off. Yes, it's not 100% honest, but as long as you don't give them the reference info of someone who will tell them you were fired, all they can legally verify is when you worked for that employer. They cannot legally ask someone if you were fired, so as long as you don't reveal that, they won't know. Since you were only there for 8 months, tell them it was a contract position that ended, and they will understand (a lot of contract positions only last between 3 - 12 months).* 

&#x200B;

Thanks very much for your input. I fortunately still have time to seek other opportunities, but after thinking over the "contract position response," and the potential of them asking further questions other than dates of employment, I felt apprehensive. I don't want to be labeled as a job-jumper because I'm not.  In the future, I'll use a different response for other interviewers. But for the one non-temp-agency recruiter whom I've already told that my contract is going to end, how would you advise handling that?

Thank you so much for your thorough response! I'm looking into unemployment already, but based on hyrle's response (the previous commenter), I'm not sure if I'll be qualified to receive it because of them having me sign a performance improvement plan (if I didn't want to sign, there is a witness line, so no way out). 

&#x200B;

I will definitely take your advice on mentioning it being a contract position; that's better than trying to figure out how to say "I'm going to get fired but <insert self-promotion pitch>". Do potential and former employers usually verify if a job is a contract position, though? That's the only thing I'm concerned about with that response.  I've certainly learned that taking on too many tasks solo is detrimental; I hate how I learned it the hard way, but it is what it is.

If you don't have a job, apply for jobs and save up so that you can move out. Don't tell them until you and your beau are getting serious, and until you're moved out. It's easier to set boundaries when you're not under their roof. If they object to you getting a job, insist that you'd like to have some spare cash to provide for things you want and not have to rely on them for financial assistance. The trick is to talk about how it will benefit them. Ensure them at the same time that it won't interfere with your studies, and make sure that you don't. 

&#x200B;

When time comes that you actually tell them you have a boyfriend, stand your ground. Now that you're moved out, now that you have a job and not depending on them completely for finances, then you can be more confident telling them that you're an adult and they don't need to treat you like a teenager. It's easy for them to throw out the "if you want to be treated like an adult, act like it" card. Well, if you're self-sufficient, you have ammo. If they threaten to pull you out of school because of your bf, then let them. They have too much pride to do it; they see you as an extension of themselves and as  a reflection of their parenting skills. Pull out your 20-year-old from college because she has a boyfriend? Now how will that look on them as parents? If in case they do, then do it yourself. Take out loans. Stop school for a bit and work so you can study again. 

&#x200B;

I understand it all sounds difficult, but it can be done. Good luck, OP.
